A||N||15||beachbomb||I did it!! I actually did it!!!||||10||
Z||000000||beachbomb||07-06-2001||10:41 PM||||With much e-mail help from Doug Kinney(fierowarehouse--thanks again--alot!!) i finally removed my first ever engine tonight!!!!! I took it out of my parts Indy. Actually i chose to lift it out of the top and not drop the cradle, mainly because of my lack of tools, all i basically have are your mainline tools, and i did it in my garage at my house! Not to toot my own horn but did all the labor by myself also. Anyway the transmission comes out next. Any good to know things about removing this before i pull it out? ANyway, just wanted to share my excitment!! I'm no longer an engine virgin!!..later..Jim( now 1 1/2 Indys)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigzach_16.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000001||KRMFiero||07-06-2001||10:48 PM||||Congrats! I found it very easy to drop the cradle it took me less then 10 hours to drop cradle/ remove engine/remove trans with only floor jacks and normal tools, to drop the cradle it probley took me 3 hours to disconnect everything and remove the bolts<P>Kyle<BR>BTW: Im 15  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red) Caddy 4.5 Project<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>       <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> <BR>AOL: Physcokyle||||reg||1
Z||000002||JayUSA77||07-06-2001||10:52 PM||||good job kyle i was only 16 when i changed my first clutch in a fiero!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>-J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||V8Fiero||07-06-2001||11:40 PM||||I have never taken an engine out of the top before but it sounds like MORE work than dropping the cradle.....<P>Heres how I do it. ( in my driveway mind you )<P>Raise the rear and place on jack stands (on the jack points right before the rear tires)<BR>Disconnect the rear suspension.<BR>Disconnect all plumbing that attatches to the car.<BR>Ive got a 3x3 dolly that I slide under the engine cradle. <BR>I then loosen the cradle bolts. Drop the back of the cradle down until it reaches the dolly<BR>Then move to the front of the cradle and do the same.<BR>At this point the entire engine/cradle assembly is sitting on the dolly and moves freely on the ground.<BR>Now its as simple as jacking the rear of the car up with a hydraulic jack and 4x4s. I use cinder blocks to put the jacks on to get it high enough to wheel the engine out the passenger side fenderwell.<P>Although this is a rough draft of how I do it, I know there are alot of other guys that do this same thing. I just have hand tools and always rent a hoist if I have to move an engine. <P>Anyway...thanks for reading.<BR>Scott<BR><P>------------------<BR>86 SE Gold<BR>86 GT Red <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RedRavensig.JPG"> <BR>"Trying to change from V8Fiero to RedRavenGT"||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierohoho||07-07-2001||12:38 AM||||Jim, good job, thats the best way to learn, hands on. Take your time, think stuff through and if you run into a problem get on Pennock's. There's nothing more satisfying than being able to say "I did it my self" and next time things go easier and faster. Plus the $ you save is good for more parts.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000005||beachbomb||07-07-2001||07:36 AM||||Thanks all, i guess it took me a total of about 10-11 hours, while i was taking it apart i was also keep all extra parts that were still usable, wiring harnesses, bolts, hoses etc. Another reason i pulled it from the top was because the frame and now that i can see the cradle good, was severly rusted, so i wasn't planning on keeping the cradle. Plus i wanted a rolling chasis so i could easily get rid of the carcus later. Anyway thanks alot for all the help i got from various forum members. Jim||||reg||1
Z||000006||chester||07-07-2001||08:31 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohoho:<BR><B>Jim, good job, thats the best way to learn, hands on. Take your time, think stuff through and if you run into a problem get on Pennock's. There's nothing more satisfying than being able to say "I did it my self" and next time things go easier and faster. Plus the $ you save is good for more parts.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The other thing is you know that the job is done right cause you did it yourself.  Sometimes its hard to tell what you'll get back from a shop.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>Congrats on your accomplishment!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">    <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension<p>[This message has been edited by chester (edited 07-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||turbo86se||07-07-2001||11:23 AM||||Good going Jim! What are your plans with the engine? You know what this means, ROAD TRIP!! <BR>Let's get together with Jeff (is he back from Kosovo?), Robert, and the bunch in North FL for a little tech session at your house! How does that sound?||||reg||1
Z||000008||SteveJ||07-07-2001||02:20 PM||||You know, its the gift that keeps on giving. I did my first engine swap about 1960 between a 50 Ford and a 53 Ford. This spring I replaced a transmission on one of the family Buicks. So what does that make it? 41 years? In that time I've saved untolled amounts and kept running cars that others threw away.<P>It really frees you to save a lot in the purchase of a car and allows you to keep 'em 'till they collapse.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||07-07-2001||03:12 PM||||I gots a question. I removed my engine buy lifting the car high enough, then using an engine hoist on the engine, unbolt everything, and lower the cradle to the ground. I always though it was the easiest way. Now I am going to be doing it again, and want to try another way. I want to unbolt the cradle and lift the up over the engine. My questions are, can I just support and unbolt the cradle, then connect my engine hoist to the car and lift it up? I'v heard of people just using their trunk latch to lift the engine-less car over the cardle? What about the rear bupmer supports, acessable by removing the rear bumper cover? Whats the easiest way to do it? <P>I'm putting a 3800 in this Fiero, so while the old engine will come out the top, I doubt the 3800 will go in the top, will it? I don't know why I ask, just curious I guess. I'm dropping the cradle anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Doni Hagan||07-09-2001||12:21 PM||||Hey, BEACHBOMB!!<P>I think I'm gonna have to pull my 2.8 from the top of my donor car as well. The thing is sitting in the field across the street on cement blocks (no front or rear suspension left.) Do you still have the e-mails from Kinney saved in your PC? If so, I'd appreciate a chance to look at them. I've got the same job to do and have never done it before, either. <P>My e-mail address is as follows. You can send the files as a text attachment. <P>Thanks a lot in advance. <BR> &lt;eclipseproduction@hotmail.com&gt;||||reg||1
Z||000011||Leadfoot||07-09-2001||12:33 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>I'v heard of people just using their trunk latch to lift the engine-less car over the cardle? What about the rear bupmer supports, acessable by removing the rear bumper cover? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I lifted the car by the inner strut mount holes.  It's much closer to the center of gravity of the body and in my opinion a safer place to lift.  I just slid some heavy duty bolts the the holes and linked it to the chain.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Leadfoot (edited 07-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||07-09-2001||10:54 PM||||This Duke owner always pulls from the top.  I take the tranny and the engine together.  <P>It's not easy, but there just is not room in my garage to slip the cradle out under the passenger side.  The top pull gets easier every time I do it.  This time, it took about four hours to strip everything and 30 minutes to pull the drivetrain, seperate the tranny, and get the engine on the stand.<P>Now, I never even think about cradle removal.  Soon, I hope to have my Duke running properly and be done with the pulling.<P>In the last three months I have pulled Dukes out the top four times.  That's enough pulling for a lifetime, if you ask me.||||reg||12
Z||000013||beachbomb||07-10-2001||12:13 PM||||Hey Joel(turbo86se), i have no problem with that!! I plan on eventually overhauling this engine then replace the one in my good Indy with it. How are things going up there, lmk, later..jim<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigzach_17.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000014||Fleabitdog||07-10-2001||04:29 PM||||I would definately drop it out the bottom. Skitime and I just pulled one from an 86 GT in the back of a junkyard two weekends ago using a fiero jack. Talk about fun. We had to jack the car up put a cinder block standing up under the car, put a cinderblock under the jack to get more height, put a rim under the cinder block standing up, and then drag the engine and cradle out the passenger side. Then we dropped the whole front suspension. I bought the GT over two months ago and the guy at the yard said they would move the car to a good place to strip it. NOT!!!!! Everyweekend he had a different excuse for not moving the car. Learned a lesson. Never give the money until car is in a good position to strip. If the engine is not drug out next weekend I am just going to strip it and carry it out in Pieces.||||reg||1
Z||000015||V8Dreamer||07-10-2001||04:57 PM||||Seanh:  You can also use the holes in the inner trunk wall/firewall, just pull the trunk liner down a bit and there are two hole you can hook a chain to.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
